title: How the help panel finds a page
tags: [[In-app help]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: The manifest, the order in which a name is resolved, the attributes that tag a zone for the help cursor, and how a page names a key without writing it.

!! The pages

The help panel's pages are the wiki's, fetched from the GUI server's `/docs/`:
`index.json` describes each page as `{id, title, file, aliases[], tags[], kind,
audience, status, summary}` — the note's fields, `id` being the slug of the title.
(The panel had older pages of its own, `panel-types/help/pages/`, merged with the
wiki's while the documentation moved; the last ones went when it was done.)

Every page is fetched when the panel is built, and its text kept for the search. The
pages are plain HTML: the wiki's renderer expands every list and macro at build
time, and the panel knows nothing of TiddlyWiki.

!! Resolving a name

`resolvePage` (`/__help.js`) turns a text into a page, or into nothing — and then
the text is searched for. Without regard to case, and in this order:

# a text starting with `#` is never a name;
# the page with that `id`, else with that `title`, else with that alias. The title comes before the aliases so that a command's own note wins over a page that lists the command among its aliases;
# for a text containing `:` — a command: the same lookup for the command without its arguments, then for what follows the colon (`metarecord-list:focus-query` reaches an alias `focus-query`), then for what precedes it (the panel type);
# `<type> panel` and `<type>` stand for each other when nothing has the exact name: `treeref` finds a note titled `treeref panel`, and `trash panel` would find a note titled `trash` if the panel had no note of its own.

`scripts/doc check` keeps the three kinds of name apart: an alias may not be the
title or the id of another note.

!! What the shell sends

`help` and `help:open` write `{topic, nonce}` to the workspace variable
`help.request` and set the focused slot's panel type to `help`. The nonce makes the
same topic asked twice count as a change. The panel reads the variable when it is
built and on each change.

The help cursor resolves a click with `resolveClickTopic`, along the click's
`composedPath()`, which goes through the panels' shadow roots:

* the nearest element with a `data-help-topic` attribute gives the topic — the shell's zones have one (`command-input`, `tab-bar`, `status-bar`, `find`…), and a panel tags its own (`finder`, `focus-query`, `columns` in `metarecord-list`);
* failing that, the nearest `data-slot-body` gives the slot, and the topic is `<panel type> panel`.

A panel type therefore gets help-cursor support by having a note named after it,
and finer answers by tagging its zones with the name or an alias of a note.

!! Key hints

A note writes `<<key "command" fallback>>`, rendered as
`<span data-mf-key="command">fallback</span>`. When it shows a page, the panel
replaces the content of each such element with the keys bound to the command, read
from `metafolder.commands.keybindings()` (`applyKeyHints`, `/__keyhints.js`):

* the attribute holds one invocation, or several separated by commas — the keys of any of them, each once, in the order of the table. That is how a row common to several panels is written;
* a bare command name matches every invocation of it (`panel:set` lists the keys of all its bindings), a fuller one only itself (`panel:set type treeref`) — the rule the command input uses to show a command's keys;
* modifiers are capitalised (`Ctrl+Shift+z`), arrows drawn as arrows, a sequence written as its keys in order (`t l`), several keys joined by ''or'';
* a command nothing is bound to reads `unbound`;
* the table is read again at each page shown.

The fallback is only what the page's source and the search index contain; it is
never displayed once the panel has the table. `scripts/doc check` refuses a hint
naming a command the GUI does not have.

A `data-mf-live="grammar"` element is filled the same way, with the current
simplified-query grammar (`<<live grammar>>`).

These functions are pure and tested in the frontend (`help.test.ts`,
`keyhints.test.ts`).
